# 🌐 IPv6 Addressing & Notation — Notes

## 📌 Overview
IPv6 (Internet Protocol version 6) replaces IPv4 and solves address exhaustion.

- IPv4 → 32-bit (~4.3 billion addresses)
- IPv6 → 128-bit (~3.4 × 10^38 addresses)

---

## 🔢 IPv6 Address Structure

- 128 bits total
- Written in hexadecimal (base-16)
- 8 groups (hextets)
- Each group = 16 bits = 4 hex digits

### Example:
```
2001:0db8:0000:0000:02aa:00ff:fe9a:4ca2
```

---

## 🔠 Hexadecimal System

| Decimal | Hex |
|--------|-----|
| 0–9    | 0–9 |
| 10–15  | A–F |

---

## ✂️ IPv6 Shortening Rules

### 1. Omit Leading Zeros
Remove leading zeros in any hextet.

```
Original:
2001:0db8:0000:0000:02aa:00ff:fe9a:4ca2

Shortened:
2001:db8:0:0:2aa:ff:fe9a:4ca2
```

---

### 2. Double Colon (::)

- Replace consecutive `0000` blocks with `::`
- Can be used only once per address

```
2001:db8:0:0:2aa:ff:fe9a:4ca2
→ 2001:db8::2aa:ff:fe9a:4ca2
```

⚠️ Important:
- Only one `::` allowed (avoids ambiguity)

---

## 🧠 Key Rules

- Always 8 hextets before compression
- Use hexadecimal (0–9, A–F)
- Remove leading zeros
- Use `::` only once
- `::` represents one or more groups of `0000`

---

## 🌍 Types of IPv6 Addresses

### 1. Unicast
- One-to-one communication

### 2. Multicast
- One-to-many communication

### 3. Anycast
- One-to-nearest communication

---

## 🧩 Address Representation

### Full Format
```
2001:0db8:0000:0000:02aa:00ff:fe9a:4ca2
```

### Shortened Format
```
2001:db8::2aa:ff:fe9a:4ca2
```

---

## ⚙️ IPv6 Prefix

```
2001:db8::/32
```

- `/32` = network portion
- Remaining bits = host portion

---

## 🚀 Why IPv6

- Huge address space
- No NAT required
- Better routing efficiency
- Built-in security (IPsec)
- Supports IoT scale

---

## 🧪 Exam Tips

- Do not use `::` more than once
- Always remove leading zeros
- Practice compressing & expanding addresses
- Know full vs shortened format

---

## 🏁 Final Takeaway

IPv6 = Hex + Compression Rules

Master the pattern → it becomes easy.
